\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A fixed Fourier mode and its massive inverse converge}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Use the [circle meshes](fdip-004H) with fixed #{\mu>0}, and fix
#{k\in\mathbb Z} before letting #{n\to\infty}. For the
[Fourier modes and eigenvalues](fdip-004I),
##{\begin{aligned}
\lambda_{n,k}&\longrightarrow4\pi^2k^2,\\
(\mu+\lambda_{n,k})^{-1}
 &\longrightarrow(\mu+4\pi^2k^2)^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
Moreover, in #{L^2(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z,dx)},
##{I_nQ_n^{-1}e_{n,k}
\longrightarrow(\mu+4\pi^2k^2)^{-1}e_k.}
The vector on the right solves #{Au=e_k} for
#{A=-d^2/dx^2+\mu} on smooth periodic functions. The selected
Gaussian moment #{\mathbb E_{\nu_n}|a_{n,k}|^2} converges to the
same inverse eigenvalue.}

\proof{
\p{For #{k=0}, the eigenvalue is identically zero and the sampled
constant interpolates exactly to #{e_0=1}; all three assertions
follow. For #{k\ne0},
##{\lambda_{n,k}
=4\pi^2k^2\left(\frac{\sin(\pi k/n)}{\pi k/n}\right)^2.}
The sine quotient tends to one. Since #{\mu>0}, all denominators
are at least #{\mu}, so continuity of inversion proves the scalar
inverse limit.}

\p{For #{j/n\leq x<(j+1)/n}, the elementary inequality
#{|e^{it}-e^{is}|\leq|t-s|} gives
##{|I_ne_{n,k}(x)-e_k(x)|\leq\frac{2\pi|k|}{n}.}
The inequality follows by integrating #{ie^{iu}} between #{s}
and #{t}. The circle has measure one, hence the same bound holds
for the #{L^2} norm. Put
#{b_n=(\mu+\lambda_{n,k})^{-1}} and
#{b=(\mu+4\pi^2k^2)^{-1}}. Because #{|b_n|\leq 1/\mu}
and #{\|e_k\|_{L^2}=1},
##{\begin{aligned}
\|b_nI_ne_{n,k}-be_k\|_{L^2}
 &\leq |b_n|\|I_ne_{n,k}-e_k\|_{L^2}+|b_n-b|\\
 &\leq \frac{2\pi|k|}{\mu n}+|b_n-b|\longrightarrow0.
\end{aligned}}
The eigenvector equation identifies the left vector with
#{I_nQ_n^{-1}e_{n,k}}. Differentiation gives
#{Ae_k=(\mu+4\pi^2k^2)e_k}. Finally the Gaussian second-moment
formula in \ref{fdip-004I} is exactly #{b_n}.}
}

\p{This is an elementary limit deduction from the preceding finite
symbol, with an explicit interpolation map. Mathlib's
\leanref{Real.tendsto_sin_div_nhdsNE_zero} supplies the nonzero
sine-quotient limit used in the proof; the zero mode was handled
separately. The declaration does not supply the interpolation or
Gaussian comparison.}
